    card title "Where do new firms come from?"

Where do new firms come from, and why do most of them die? Start with every new American business site that opened in 1994. A site can be a new firm, or a new branch of an old one.
    draw axes

B: Over half a million of them: sh (@draw line_3@)ops, cafés, garages, workshops, all open for business in the same year.

Each starts at the top of this chart: a hundred percent still open.
    highlight line_3 effect circle

B: The usual story is that most die in their first year. I'd have guessed that too.
    point at.data [1,15] gesture circle

    ask "Draw the share still open, from opening day to year 20." on line_1 from 0.01 store g
        intro: Your turn: draw how many you think were still open, year by year, for twenty years.
        right: Four in five after a year, one in five after twenty: your line was about {g.off} points off.
        wrong: Your line was about {g.off} points off: here, four in five after a year, one in five after twenty.

After one year, about eig (@draw c80@)hty percent were still open. Only one in five had closed.

B: Then it keeps leaking. Hal (@draw c50@)f were gone by year five, two  (@draw c34@)thirds by year ten.

Afte (@draw c20@)r twenty years, one in five was left. Not a cliff in year one: a slow, steady leak.
    erase c80 c50

B: So most die. But look at what happened to their jobs.
    erase c34 c20 line_3

After ten y (@draw line_2@)ears, a third of the businesses still held almost three quarter (@draw c73@)s of the jobs they started with.

The ones that close are mostly tiny. The ones tha (@draw size_box size_line_1 size_line_2@)t live grow: from about seven workers on average to sixteen.

Up or out: most new sites are small experiments that fai (@highlight line_1 line_2@)l cheaply, and the few that work grow.
    erase size c73

    pause 0.4

## Young, not small
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Share of all jobs
      - Share of new jobs
    values:
      - 1.9
      - 15.5
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 20
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 1
    y_label: First-year US firms, 2019
    label_size: 26
sources:
    - id: bds
      title: 'Business Dynamics Statistics: Firm Age, 1978–2023 (bds2023_fa)'
      authors: US Census Bureau
      year: 2025
      finding: In 2019 firms aged 0 held 1.9% of US private employment and accounted for 15.5% of gross job creation (2.45 of 15.8 million jobs created).
      url: https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/bds/tables/time-series/2023/bds2023_fa.csv
    - id: hjm
      title: Who Creates Jobs? Small versus Large versus Young
      authors: John Haltiwanger, Ron S. Jarmin, Javier Miranda
      year: 2013
      finding: 'Review of Economics and Statistics 95(2): 347–361. Once firm age is controlled for, there is no systematic relationship between firm size and growth; business start-ups and young businesses are important to US job creation.'
      doi: 10.1162/REST_a_00288
    - id: schump
      title: Capitalism, Socialism and Democracy
      authors: Joseph A. Schumpeter
      year: 1942
      finding: 'Coins ''creative destruction'': the process that incessantly revolutionizes the economic structure from within, destroying the old one and creating a new one.'

    text twist "Age, not size" #b5482e cites ["hjm"] x 740 y 560 font_size 44 hidden true
    cards traits check each hidden true
        Young firms: Often closes, Grows fast if it lives, Holds few jobs
        Old firms: Holds most jobs, Closes less often, Grows slowly
    box weak "Weak firm shrinks" x 175 y 440 width 270 height 140 radius 10 font_size 36 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    box move "Workers move" x 500 y 440 width 250 height 140 radius 10 font_size 36 hidden true
    box strong "Strong firm grows" x 825 y 440 width 270 height 140 radius 10 font_size 36 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
    arrow e1 from.anchor right from.ref weak to.anchor left to.ref move hidden true
    arrow e2 from.anchor right from.ref move to.anchor left to.ref strong hidden true
    mark stop around move kind cross hidden true
    text cd "creative destruction" #3d3833 cites ["schump"] x 500 y 270 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "Young, not small"

If the survivors grow, are new firms where the jobs come from?
    draw axes

B: Take American firms in their very first year. I (@draw bar_1@)n 2019 they held about two percent of all jobs.

    ask "Drag the bar: their share of new jobs." on bar_2 store g default 3 stage own
        intro: What share of all the new jobs created that year do you think they made? Drag the second bar.
        right: {g.true}%: you said {g}%, close.
        wrong: You said {g}%: here, {g.true}%.

B: About fifteen percent. Firms with two percent of the jobs made about one in six of the new ones.
    highlight bar_2

So the old slogan is right: small business creates the jobs?
    point at.ref bar_1 gesture tap

B: That's the trap. Haltiwanger, Jarmin and Miranda compared firms of the same age. Then size made no (@draw twist@) systematic difference to growth.

B: It's young firms that grow fast, if they survive. A corner shop open for thirty years is small too, and mostly stays that way.
    highlight twist effect underline

    erase axes bar_1 bar_2 twist

    draw traits

    ask "Drop each trait on young firms or old firms." on traits store f stage own
        intro: Sort these for me: which traits belong to young firms, and which to old ones?
        right: All six. Young firms live fast; old firms hold the jobs.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total}: young churn, old hold jobs.

B: Young firms close often and grow fast. Old firms hold most of the jobs and change slowly.
    highlight traits

    erase traits

And when (@draw weak e1 move@) a weak firm closes, its workers and customers move to stronger ones.

That's reallocation again, workers moving to the better firm, and it's much of how average productivity rises.
    draw e2 strong

B: Now imagine a rule that kept every fir (@draw stop@)m alive: the workers stay stuck where they produce least.

It's Schumpeter's creative (@draw cd@) destruction, here at the level of whole firms.
    erase stop

So the dying isn't pure waste: it's ho (@highlight weak move strong@)w the few good ideas get room to grow.

    pause 0.4

## Too many, or too few?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Haltiwanger would hire you.
    good: Most founders would envy those odds.
    poor: 'Like most startups: try again.'
    none: Even entrepreneurs skip the market research.
