sources:
    - id: cwd
      title: Entrepreneurs' perceived chances for success
      authors: Arnold C. Cooper, Carolyn Y. Woo, William C. Dunkelberg
      year: 1988
      finding: 'Journal of Business Venturing 3(2): 97–108. Of 2,994 new business owners, 81% put their own odds of success at 7 in 10 or better, and 33% at 10 in 10.'
    - id: cl
      title: 'Overconfidence and Excess Entry: An Experimental Approach'
      authors: Colin Camerer, Dan Lovallo
      year: 1999
      finding: 'American Economic Review 89(1): 306–318. Subjects entered a market more when payoffs depended on their rank in a skill test than when rank was random, especially those who self-selected into the skill condition (reference group neglect).'
      doi: 10.1257/aer.89.1.306
    - id: bds
      title: 'Business Dynamics Statistics: Firm Age, 1978–2023 (bds2023_fa)'
      authors: US Census Bureau
      year: 2025
      finding: 'Firms aged 0 as a share of all firms: 13.7% in 1978, 7.6% in 2010, 9.1% in 2023.'
      url: https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/bds/tables/time-series/2023/bds2023_fa.csv
    - id: kps
      title: Demographic Origins of the Startup Deficit
      authors: Fatih Karahan, Benjamin Pugsley, Ayşegül Şahin
      year: 2024
      finding: 'American Economic Review 114(7): 1986–2023. The slowdown in labour-supply growth since the late 1970s, largely predetermined by demographics, explains roughly half of the decline in the US startup rate.'
      doi: 10.1257/aer.20210362
    - id: aa
      title: What Happened to US Business Dynamism?
      authors: Ufuk Akcigit, Sina T. Ates
      year: 2023
      finding: 'Journal of Political Economy 131(8): 2059–2124. A decline in knowledge diffusion from frontier firms to laggards is the dominant driver of falling business dynamism and rising concentration.'
      doi: 10.1086/724289
    - id: hjm
      title: Who Creates Jobs? Small versus Large versus Young
      authors: John Haltiwanger, Ron S. Jarmin, Javier Miranda
      year: 2013
      finding: 'Review of Economics and Statistics 95(2): 347–361. Controlling for age, firm size has no systematic relationship with growth; young firms matter for job creation.'
      doi: 10.1162/REST_a_00288

    text many "Too many?" #b5482e x 500 y 610 font_size 46 hidden true
    scratch survey cites ["cwd","cl"] work ["New owners, 1988","81%: my odds ≥ 7 in 10","33%: 10 in 10","Lab: more entry when skill decides"] x 500 y 420 font_size 32 hidden true
    text few "Too few?" #2f6b8f x 850 y 610 font_size 46 hidden true
