    text s2b "contested" #b5482e x 860 y 288 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Too many, or too few?"

B: Churn makes us richer, so more new firms must be better. Or a (@draw many@)re there already too many?

Too many, says one camp. Mos (@draw survey_box survey_line_1@)t founders are sure they'll beat the odds.

In a 1988 survey of nearly three thousand new business owners, eighty-on (@draw survey_line_2@)e percent put their own chances at seven in ten or better. One i (@draw survey_line_3@)n three said ten in ten.

B: In the lab, Camerer and Lovallo fo (@draw survey_line_4@)und more people entered a market when winners were picked by skill rather than luck: each forgot the rivals thought they were skilled too.

    erase survey many

Too few, sa (@draw few@)ys the other camp. He (@draw axes line_1@)re is America's startup rate: the share of all firms in their first year.

In 1978 almost f (@draw r78@)ourteen percent of firms were brand new. By (@draw r10@) 2010, under eight.

B: It has crept back since, to about nine p (@draw r23@)ercent in 2023. Still well below where it began.

Why it fell is contested. One study blames a slower- (@draw why_box why_line_1 why_line_2@)growing workforce; another, leading firms pulling ah (@draw why_line_3@)ead as ideas spread more slowly to the rest.
    erase few

B: So both can be true at once: each founder too hopeful, and the eco (@highlight line_1@)nomy getting fewer founders than it used to.
    erase why

How sure are we? That young firms d (@draw s1 s1b@)rive job growth by up or out: mostly agreed. Whether Amer (@draw s2 s2b@)ica now has too little entry, and why: contested.

    quiz "A government cuts taxes for every firm with under 20 workers, to create jobs. What does the evidence suggest?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: It mostly misses. Size alone doesn't predict growth; young firms of any size do the growing.
        wrong: Compare a corner shop open for thirty years with a two-year-old firm of the same size: here, it mostly misses.
        * It works: small firms create most jobs
        + It mostly misses: the growth is in young firms
        * It works only in a recession

    quiz "A town grants only five café licences, and the holders never lose them. What happens to its cafés' average quality over twenty years?" voice b
        right: It falls behind: no newcomer can test a better idea, and no weak café has to make room.
        wrong: Ask who can enter, and who ever has to leave: here, it falls behind.
        * It rises
        + It falls behind
        * Nothing changes

    quiz "An app store ranks apps by quality, and every developer thinks theirs is above average. What do you expect?"
        right: Too many enter. Each forgets the rivals are just as sure of themselves, so most lose money.
        wrong: The rivals are just as sure: here, too many enter.
        + Too many enter, and most lose money
        * Exactly the right number enter
        * Too few enter

So where do new firms come from? From people willing to bet on an idea, most of them wrongly. Their losses are what it costs to find the few firms that make everyone richer.
    highlight line_1

    pause 0.5

